3. THE CLOCK TEST for m (the bound translated to the keV scale)

   if m were a varying constant at the |p| <= 6e-8 level, the e/m
   ratios in atomic clocks would drift.  The bound on the germ:

     |delta m/m| per year <= |p| * H0 t_yr = 4.136e-18 /yr
     |dm/dt| <= 5.09 keV * 4.136e-18 = 2.105e-17 keV/yr
             = 2.105e-14 eV/yr
     over a Hubble time (13.8 Gyr) at the bound, the TOTAL possible
     drift is 2.905e-07 keV -- a part in 1.8e+07 of m.

   The construction satisfies this bound IDENTICALLY: the freeze
   relation m sigma^2 = k_B T_0(1+z*) pins m at every epoch (section 2b),
   so the e/m ratios in atomic clocks cannot drift -- m is
   constant-by-construction, not merely bounded below the clock.

